{"subjectId":"c","id":"binary-search-tree","title":"Binary Search Tree","group":"Data Structures & Algorithms in C","order":44,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">A binary tree where every left descendant is smaller and every right descendant is larger than the node. Search, insert and delete are O(h) — O(log n) when balanced, O(n) when degenerate.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n#include &lt;stdlib.h&gt;\n\ntypedef struct Tree { int key; struct Tree *left, *right; } Tree;\n\nTree *insert(Tree *t, int key) {\n    if (!t) {\n        t = malloc(sizeof *t);\n        if (!t) exit(1);\n        t-&gt;key = key; t-&gt;left = t-&gt;right = NULL;\n    } else if (key &lt; t-&gt;key) t-&gt;left = insert(t-&gt;left, key);\n    else if (key &gt; t-&gt;key)   t-&gt;right = insert(t-&gt;right, key);\n    return t;                                  /* duplicates ignored */\n}\n\nconst Tree *search(const Tree *t, int key) {\n    while (t &amp;&amp; t-&gt;key != key) t = key &lt; t-&gt;key ? t-&gt;left : t-&gt;right;\n    return t;\n}\n\nTree *delete(Tree *t, int key) {\n    if (!t) return NULL;\n    if (key &lt; t-&gt;key) t-&gt;left = delete(t-&gt;left, key);\n    else if (key &gt; t-&gt;key) t-&gt;right = delete(t-&gt;right, key);\n    else if (!t-&gt;left || !t-&gt;right) {          /* 0 or 1 child */\n        Tree *child = t-&gt;left ? t-&gt;left : t-&gt;right;\n        free(t);\n        return child;\n    } else {                                   /* 2 children: copy in-order successor */\n        Tree *s = t-&gt;right;\n        while (s-&gt;left) s = s-&gt;left;\n        t-&gt;key = s-&gt;key;\n        t-&gt;right = delete(t-&gt;right, s-&gt;key);\n    }\n    return t;\n}\n\nvoid inorder(const Tree *t)  { if (t) { inorder(t-&gt;left); printf(\"%d \", t-&gt;key); inorder(t-&gt;right); } }\nvoid preorder(const Tree *t) { if (t) { printf(\"%d \", t-&gt;key); preorder(t-&gt;left); preorder(t-&gt;right); } }\nint height(const Tree *t) {\n    if (!t) return 0;\n    int l = height(t-&gt;left), r = height(t-&gt;right);\n    return 1 + (l &gt; r ? l : r);\n}\nvoid destroy(Tree *t) { if (t) { destroy(t-&gt;left); destroy(t-&gt;right); free(t); } }  /* postorder */\n\nint main(void) {\n    Tree *root = NULL;\n    int keys[] = {50, 30, 70, 20, 40, 60, 80};\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; 7; i++) root = insert(root, keys[i]);\n\n    printf(\"inorder:  \"); inorder(root);  printf(\"\\n\");\n    printf(\"preorder: \"); preorder(root); printf(\"\\n\");\n    printf(\"height %d, has 60? %s, has 65? %s\\n\", height(root),\n           search(root, 60) ? \"yes\" : \"no\", search(root, 65) ? \"yes\" : \"no\");\n\n    root = delete(root, 50);                   /* root with two children */\n    printf(\"after deleting 50: \"); inorder(root); printf(\"(root %d)\\n\", root-&gt;key);\n    destroy(root);\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>inorder:  20 30 40 50 60 70 80\npreorder: 50 30 20 40 70 60 80\nheight 3, has 60? yes, has 65? no\nafter deleting 50: 20 30 40 60 70 80 (root 60)</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Traversal</th><th>Order</th><th>Use</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>In-order</td><td>left, node, right</td><td>Sorted output of a BST</td></tr><tr><td>Pre-order</td><td>node, left, right</td><td>Copy / serialise a tree</td></tr><tr><td>Post-order</td><td>left, right, node</td><td>Free a tree (children before parent)</td></tr><tr><td>Level-order</td><td>breadth-first with a queue</td><td>Print by levels, shortest path in unweighted trees</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>Inserting already-sorted keys produces a linked list (height n) and O(n) operations. Self-balancing trees (AVL, red-black) keep the height O(log n).</p></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How do you check whether a binary tree is a valid BST?</summary><div><p>Recurse with an allowed range: every node’s key must lie strictly between a lower and upper bound inherited from its ancestors (<code>check(node-&gt;left, lo, node-&gt;key)</code>, <code>check(node-&gt;right, node-&gt;key, hi)</code>). Comparing a node only with its direct children is not enough.</p></div></details>"}